{"name": "NIH/NHGRI sole-source Illumina reagent IDIQ 75N98026D00035 ($90M ceiling, May 2026–May 2031) and its task orders","url": "https://www.highergov.com/idv/75N98026D00035/","access": "I opened this page. It shows Illumina as vendor (UEI KMW3CRWNAAF3), NIH OLAO as awarding office, NHGRI as funder, a $90M ceiling, ~$5.35M (6%) obligated, PoP start 21 May 2026, last modified 8 Oct 2026, 'Sole Source / Unique Source', and one bid. The description 'Under Eo 14398' only refers to the DEI contract clause. Next steps: (1) On USAspending.gov (no login), search the award ID 75N98026D00035 and pull every child delivery order (75N9xxxx…), with obligation date, ordering institute and description. (2) Search SAM.gov or its mirrors (sweetspotgov, govtribe snippets, highergov opportunity pages) for the pre-solicitation and J&A, using the queries 'Illumina Reagents and Consumables' NIH 2026 'notice of intent' and '75N98026'. The NIH pre-solicitation for NovaSeq X 25B kits (response due 7 May 2026, seen on g2xchange) is probably its precursor. (3) Compare against the FY24–FY25 per-institute Illumina purchase-order history on the same profile.","labels": ["2B","2A","1B"],"rationale": "NIH appears to have moved its intramural Illumina consumables buying into a single 5-year, NHGRI-run sole-source vehicle. That turns many small research buyers into one large account, which is exactly the 'large labs push for bigger discounts' mechanism. The J&A or price-reasonableness memo for a $90M sole-source deal usually cites the discount off list or the GSA price. Task-order run-rate also gives a clean volume read: ~$5.35M in ~4.5 months annualizes to ~$14M/yr, which can be compared with the prior scattered NIH spend. If the ceiling or annualized spend is flat to down while kit mix shifts to 25B, that is research volume flat and $/Gb down.","expected_payoff": "high"},
{"name": "Federal contractor-profile aggregators: Element Biosciences vs Illumina award series by fiscal year (USDA ARS, HHS/NIH)","url": "https://govconinabox.com/explore/contractors/profile/element-biosciences-inc-cz2che","access": "I opened the Element profile. It lists 13 awards: FY22 $395K, FY23 $614K, FY24 $509K, FY25 $782K, mostly USDA AVITI systems plus a $198K AVITI cartridge buy in Sep 2025. The headline says $3.46M across 24 awards, so the list is incomplete. The Illumina profile (https://govconinabox.com/explore/contractors/profile/illumina-inc-kmw3cr) shows $169.1M across 2,848 awards to Dec 2025, as a search snippet. For the full series, use USAspending.gov award search (no login) with recipient = 'Element Biosciences' and recipient = 'Illumina, Inc.', award type = contracts, FY2022–FY2026, grouped by awarding sub-agency and PSC 6640. Pull the description text to separate instruments, reagents and service.","labels": ["2B","2A"],"rationale": "This is a clean, dated record of federal research buyers choosing a rival platform, and of what they pay for its consumables. If USDA/NIH Illumina reagent obligations are flat or down in FY25–26 while Element cartridge buys and AVITI placements rise, that is volume flat for Illumina in research with share bleeding to cheaper platforms. Matching item descriptions (e.g., 'NovaSeq reagent kits' at ~$143K, Sep 2025) can also give unit prices.","expected_payoff": "medium"},
{"name": "UT Austin GSAF Confluence wiki 'Sequencing Prices and Descriptions': page-version history","url": "https://cloud.wikis.utexas.edu/wiki/x/eRBPAg","access": "Seen as a search-result snippet; not opened. Open the page, then try the Confluence page-history view (Page tools > Page History, or /wiki/pages/viewpreviousversions.action?pageId=<id>). Many public university Confluence spaces show prior versions without login. Diff the NovaSeq X 10B/25B, NovaSeq 6000 and any AVITI prices across versions dated 2024–2026.","labels": ["2B","1B"],"rationale": "UT Austin's core is not in the corpus. Confluence keeps every dated revision, which gives a true price time series rather than a single snapshot. It would show whether research-facing lane prices fell (or rose for tariffs) in 2025–26, and whether internal and external rates diverged. This is a forensic route around the blocked Wayback access.","expected_payoff": "medium"},
{"name": "WordPress REST API on core-facility sites: hidden 'modified' dates and revision history of pricing pages (NCSU GSL, Johns Hopkins IGC, UGA GGBC)","url": "https://research.ncsu.edu/gsl/wp-json/wp/v2/pages/1622","access": "The NCSU wp-json URL and https://igc.jhmi.edu/?p=14200 came up in search; neither was opened. For each WordPress-based core: GET /wp-json/wp/v2/pages?search=novaseq and /wp-json/wp/v2/posts?search=price, then read the 'date', 'modified' and 'content.rendered' fields. Also try /wp-json/wp/v2/pages/<id>/revisions (often public on misconfigured sites). JHMI IGC shows a 10B lane at '$1200' at 300 cycles (snippet). Run the same routine on dna.uga.edu (WordPress).","labels": ["2B"],"rationale": "Johns Hopkins IGC is a new core, and its $1,200 per 10B lane would sit below Utah ($1,250) and UCLA ($1,766). The REST API gives exact modification dates, and sometimes earlier text, for price pages, so it can confirm a 2026 cut without Wayback. A cluster of 2026 'modified' dates on lower X lane prices would quantify research realized-price erosion at the core level.","expected_payoff": "medium"},
{"name": "University of Georgia GGBC news archive (pages 2–5): dated price-change announcements","url": "https://dna.uga.edu/category/news","access": "I opened page 1. The 12 Mar 2025 post 'Spring Forward With Lower Prices' lists X Plus lanes at $2,000 (10B) and $3,000 (25B), DNA libraries at $20–45 per sample and RNA libraries at $50–80, but does not give the prior prices. Next: open https://dna.uga.edu/category/news/page/2/ through /page/5/ and https://dna.uga.edu/2025/03, find the earlier price post (likely 2023–24, 6000 or the first X rates), and look for any post after Mar 2025.","labels": ["2B","2A"],"rationale": "The Georgia core is already an expert source (flat volume only because of a contract; 2025 ~−20%), but its own published price history is not in the corpus. A dated before-and-after on lane and library prices would document the research price cut that went along with the flat-to-down volume the expert described.","expected_payoff": "medium"},
{"name": "MD Anderson Advanced Technology Genomics Core: services-and-fees schedule ('up to 60% reduction in sequencing price')","url": "https://www.mdanderson.org/research/research-resources/core-facilities/advanced-technology-genomics-core/services-and-fees/novaseqx-plus-sequencing.html","access": "I opened the page. It says 'Up to 60% reduction in sequencing price (depending upon sequencing type)' but gives no prices or date. Earlier search snippets showed $2,595 per 25B lane and $1,523 per 10B lane at 300 cycles. Next: open the parent services-and-fees.html and the legacy PDF https://mdanderson.org/documents/core-facilities/sequencing-and-microarray-facility/NovaSeqOpening.pdf (6000-era prices) and compare.","labels": ["2B","1A"],"rationale": "This is a new core: a large cancer-center research core with both 6000-era and X-era fee schedules. The 6000-to-X step at research cores is known, but MD Anderson's 25B lane at $2,595 (~$2.0–2.6/Gb, depending on read assumption) would be among the lowest academic rates. That fits continued research price erosion beyond the conversion step.","expected_payoff": "low"},
{"name": "UC campus 'Approved Recharge Rate List' PDFs (UCSC and sister campuses), 2025-26 vs 2026-27 genomics lines","url": "https://planning.ucsc.edu/budget/rates-and-assessments/recharge-rates/2025-26-Approved-Recharge-Rate-List-6.30.25.pdf","access": "Seen as a search-result snippet; not opened. Fetch the 2025-26 list, then guess the 2026-27 equivalent (same folder, '2026-27-Approved-Recharge-Rate-List-6.30.26.pdf' or a similar name) and the 2024-25 list. Extract the genomics core, sequencing and NovaSeq lines. Repeat for UC Davis, UC Riverside and UCSD budget-office recharge lists, using the query site:ucdavis.edu OR site:ucr.edu 'approved recharge rates' 2026-27 sequencing.","labels": ["2B"],"rationale": "Campus-wide approved-rate lists are formal, dated, multi-year documents. That differs from the single-memo search that came up dry under S588. They show whether sequencing recharge rates fell, held or rose into FY27, a direct read on research realized price. Rate holds despite 5% tariff surcharges would also be informative.","expected_payoff": "low"}
